      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Description: The left coronary artery (LCA) is one of the main arteries
    supplying blood to the heart. It branches off from the aorta just above the aortic
    valve.



    Location: Originating from the aorta''s left sinus of Valsalva,
    the LCA courses along the heart''s left anterior surface.



    Function: The
    left coronary artery divides into two primary branches: the left anterior descending
    (LAD) artery and the left circumflex (LCX) artery. These branches supply oxygen-rich
    blood to the left side of the heart, including the left ventricle and the left
    atrium, ensuring the heart muscle receives the necessary nutrients to function
    effectively.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
