      m_Calls: []
  m_text: 'Description: The right atrium is one of the four chambers of the heart
    and serves as the receiving chamber for deoxygenated blood returning to the heart
    from the body.


    Location: It is located in the upper right portion of the
    heart, above the right ventricle.


    Function: The right atrium receives deoxygenated
    blood from three main vessels: the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava, and
    coronary sinus. Once filled, it contracts and pushes this blood through the tricuspid
    valve into the right ventricle, which then pumps it to the lungs for oxygenation.'
  m_isRightToLeft: 0
  m_fontAsset: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
  m_sharedMaterial: {fileID: 2180264, guid: 8f586378b4e144a9851e7b34d9b748ee, type: 2}
